# Security and Privacy

## Scope

This policy covers the `{{PROJECT_TITLE}}` repository, its build, generated outputs, local services, and selected deployment.

## Trust boundaries

- Canonical Markdown, source records, web content, prompts, tool responses, and generated artifacts are untrusted inputs to tooling.
- Source-provided instructions must not execute. Rendering and extraction must not execute source code, active documents, or embedded instructions by default.
- LLM answers, rendered pages, graph edges, and generated summaries have no independent evidence authority.
- HTML or other active content derived from a source or model must be sanitized or rendered as non-executable text.

## Required controls

- Secrets stay outside Git, URLs, generated pages, fixtures, logs, and screenshots; use the chosen platform's secret store.
- State-changing tools, external publication, and write-capable integrations require explicit authorization and least-privilege credentials.
- Personal or confidential data is excluded, redacted before entry, or kept in a separately declared access-controlled store.
- Deletion and correction propagate from the authoritative source record to affected claims, concepts, and regenerated outputs without retaining deleted sensitive content in logs.
- Dependencies remain locked and audited; generators write only to declared derived paths.
- Managed previews bind to loopback, reserve endpoints through the shared local register, reject unmanaged listeners, and expose only the non-secret static service identity marker.
- Standard proposal records and previews contain safe summaries and bounded references only. Reject secrets, private absolute paths, personal or confidential material, restricted evidence, public vulnerability detail, and unlicensed content; never upload consumer files or attachments automatically.
- Proposal validation and rendering are local-only. A governed-form open performs read-only remote checks and leaves final submission to the authorised user; no CLI fallback may create an issue, publish a release, or change a consumer pin.
- Report suspected vulnerabilities or exposed personal data through `{{SECURITY_REPORTING_CHANNEL}}`, never a public issue.

## Supported revisions

Security fixes apply to `{{SUPPORTED_REVISION_POLICY}}`.
